WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.082
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.082 --> 00:00:04.646
<v Examiner>Compare health equality and health equity.

3
00:00:04.646 --> 00:00:09.409
<v Examiner>Then explain the difference between a health inequality and a health inequity,

4
00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:12.034
<v Examiner>using an example from Aotearoa New Zealand.

5
00:00:12.484 --> 00:00:15.586
Compare, so name both, then say how they relate.

6
00:00:15.586 --> 00:00:18.041
Equality means everyone gets the same.

7
00:00:18.041 --> 00:00:23.340
Equity means fairness: resources go out according to need, so that the gap closes.

8
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:27.993
Lisa's picture is the people of different heights watching over a fence.

9
00:00:27.993 --> 00:00:30.643
Equality gives each of them the same box.

10
00:00:30.643 --> 00:00:35.166
Equity gives the shortest person more boxes, so that everyone can see.

11
00:00:35.166 --> 00:00:40.465
They relate because equal treatment of unequal starting points keeps the gap open.

12
00:00:40.465 --> 00:00:42.210
Equity is how you close it.

13
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:43.940
Now the second pair.

14
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:48.294
A health inequality is simply a difference in health between groups.

15
00:00:48.294 --> 00:00:50.471
Some differences are just biology.

16
00:00:50.471 --> 00:00:54.504
A health inequity is a difference that is unfair and avoidable.

17
00:00:54.504 --> 00:00:59.690
It comes from the social conditions people are born, grow, live, work and age in,

18
00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:03.787
and it could be reduced by the right mix of government policies.

19
00:01:03.787 --> 00:01:08.461
The test is her question on the slide: are these differences fair or not?

20
00:01:08.911 --> 00:01:11.575
An example from Aotearoa, from her deck.

21
00:01:11.575 --> 00:01:17.104
Māori and Pacific adults are about twice as likely as other adults not to collect a

22
00:01:17.104 --> 00:01:19.036
prescription because of cost,

23
00:01:19.036 --> 00:01:24.232
and Māori adults have higher hospitalisation rates for diabetes complications,

24
00:01:24.232 --> 00:01:27.297
like kidney failure and lower-limb amputation.

25
00:01:27.297 --> 00:01:28.562
That's not biology.

26
00:01:28.562 --> 00:01:33.292
It follows from barriers to access: cost, transport, appointment times.

27
00:01:33.292 --> 00:01:39.088
Unfair and avoidable, so it is an inequity, and the nurse's response is an equity lens:

28
00:01:39.088 --> 00:01:42.818
work out what this person needs to get the same outcome.

29
00:01:43.268 --> 00:01:45.372
Equality: everyone gets the same.

30
00:01:45.372 --> 00:01:48.815
Equity: fairness, resources by need, to close the gap.

31
00:01:48.815 --> 00:01:52.130
Inequality: any difference in health between groups.

32
00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:56.975
Inequity: a difference that is unfair and avoidable, from social conditions,

33
00:01:56.975 --> 00:01:59.270
and fixable with the right policies.

34
00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:04.817
Example: Māori and Pacific adults twice as likely not to collect a prescription because

35
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:05.327
of cost.
